      "text": "Because the Timaeus deals pervasively with the question of beginnings, it is appropriate to see it also as a recommencement of the task that was central to the Republic, which is to bring Athens back to its ideal form (at the time of the dialogue, apparently, the interlocutors saw it as less than it had been). What we learn from the Republic is that while the ideal city they describe evokes powerful feelings and approval, it remains an ideal. It misses actuality, and the discussants hope this condition is rectifiable, that this city ideal can be brought to life and seen vigorously exercising as states do (Timaeus 18b). As it is, the ideal city is a dead city. Sallis remarks that, strictly speaking, it is a \"technical city,\" a city of the head achieved through philosophical technique (20). Not only is it a fabricated artifact of thought, but it lacks eros, which is to say, it lacks becoming in a generative sense. The fact that the Republic focuses strongly on controlling eros and all that goes with it, such as \"procreation, mating, birth, sexual difference, [and] corporeity itself in its singularity,\" takes on greater significance in this context (Sallis 26). There is thus a tension between control as something intellectual and life as something that exceeds the intellectual. For this reason, among others, Sallis tells us that from the very beginning, the Timaeus comments on the limits of fabrication, whether as techne or poiesis, with respect to eros (26). This is very much a problem of invention in the sense of finding ways to actualize or enact what are initially only ideas, feelings, or intuitions. That is, the Platonic chora addresses the question of the available means of creation and how we give life to and make a place for (static) ideas. Furthermore, the discussion implicitly evokes a distinction\n\nbetween the made and the born, or between techni and physis. The chora gains even greater import when we see that it emerges precisely to dissolve such distinctions and give life to artifice.\n\nFriedrich Solmsen argues that we can understand Plato to be wrestling with a problem that reaches far back into debates carried on by the pre-Socratics. How is one to understand genesis, or becoming? Parmenides is held to have dismissed any outright genesis; Empedocles refined Parmenides's thought, suggesting instead that something akin t omixture, achieved through the interactions of the four elements or roots (see chapter 7), could replace genesis. The Timaeus itself addresses this issue, but it does so as a reopening of the question of becoming. Throughout the dialogue, the uncertainty of knowing anything about genesis is remarked, and even the accounts given are characterized as only probable or likely (Timaeus 28b, 44d, 48c-d, 55d, 59c. 66d). ln this regard, the ch6ra can be taken as Plato's attempt to reintroduce the notion of becoming, even ifhe steps back from the more assertive positions of his predecessors (Solmsen 40). At the risk of oversimplifying, we might say that Plato posited this refined notion of genesis as an aid in thinking through how, as Algra puts it, the sensible world participates in the intelligible world (3). Ann Ashbaugh makes a similar point when she claims that the Timaeus was the mast important Platonic dialogue in antiquity because it addressed a fundamental question, one that we have yet to answer definitively: not \"what an objective cosmos is, but how it comes to be known by the souln (1). Apropos ofSallis's comment on the limits of fabrication, the Republic already carries in germinal form this very question: haw does one move from ideas, or the realm of the Forms. to the sensible, active world?\n\nThis sensible, active world is not one of simple presence or mundane materiality. The Greeks, like other ancient peoples, had a strong sense of what remains hidden, obscure, and withdrawn in the world. Indeed. the ancient sense of the divine was itself tethered to place, as E. V. Walter argues throughout his book Placeways. Thus, we can see Plato's tum to myth (which pervades his dialogues), his evocation of a\"bastard reasoning,\" as bringing together his desire for rationality with his more obscure leanings toward doctrines espoused by initiates of divine mysteries, such as thos e of the Pythagorean and Orphic traditions (see Kingsley). His notion of the ch6ra evokes this duality, since it cortjoins rationalist explanation. including the mathematics of the triangles in the latter half of the dialogue (which has an ancient legacy, older than the Pythagoreans, from whom he presumably learned it), with more ephemeral qualities of place, such as the",
