      "text": "discussion of the fourfold discharges any such faith in representation. That is, we can understand things, or nonhuman entities, as represented objects only via an already limited grant of inclusion. Such entry is tightly circumscribed and controlled by a reinscription of the human. Nonhuman actants certainly have agency, as Latour details, but when it comes to a politics that addresses how we are to dwell together with nonhuman actants, their role is circumscribed as one of already present things that have no claims on us beyond that about which they obtain acknowledgment concerning their immediate presence and the alliances they form. One telling way in which the emphasis on presence matters can be seen in the extent to which Latour's parliament of things depends on an a priori separation between humans and nonhumans. We know in advance which is which and where their boundaries lie. In many respects this is an odd position for Latour; after all, he, like others, wants to dissolve clear boundaries so as to extend the body. But we might recall Andy Clark's argument that human beings are from the beginning biotechnological hybrids, resiliently plastic in conjoining themselves with the features and things of the world (Natural 134-36). In many respects, then, it is odd that the parliament of things simultaneously and paradoxically acknowledges the power of human-nonhuman assemblages only to maintain which is which. Perhaps a better beginning point would be the idea that all \"parliaments,\" while they are gatherings about contentious things, are of course already cobbled together with things, which is to say that parliamentary bodies are deeply plastic biotechnological hybrids. If this idea were pursued, however, the issue of representation would surely show up differently. Certainly Karen Barad's critique of Latour is pertinent here, for Barad points out that the \"political field is not limited to the statehouse\"; rather, part of what constitutes the political field is the ongoing activity that separates human and nonhuman, those represented and those not represented, and those who act and those who do not (59).\n\nCompared to Heidegger's use of the fourfold, Latour's excessive focus on presence and the known must seem problematic. More troubling still is that, if such a shift is going to transform human life, say, to a more ecological attunement, the claims of nonhuman actants will be reduced to their already present pulsions and recalcitrances. The richer understanding of how things condition us not simply as present and revealed objects but as the essential possibility for any dwelling at all seems lost in Latour's work. Put more simply, a representation-based parliament of things must fail because it cannot reach far enough into how we dwell, into seeing, perhaps, how the conditions of possibility for thinking a parliament of things is al-\n\nready generated within our current relations with things. How things show up for us in a technological age, Heidegger might say, is already involved in and thus crucially shapes our thinking, including ideas such as Latour's human-nonhuman parliament. Latour's argument betrays a sense of exhortation-a \"we need to do this\"-that traces an assumption that we can simply revalue so as to garner representation for nonhumans. Such an argument cannot consider what the fourfold teaches us: things are not simply present and relational, awaiting representation; rather, they constitute the impossibly convoluted and interactive stitchwork ofbeing to which we have only partial access. The fourfold, then, is a further thinking about the withdrawn chorographic background from which dwelling gains its bearings.\n\nWe cannot simply and directly choose to dwell otherwise. A new attunement is necessary, one that will spring from preparatory work across the full range of human dwelling, and this attunement must ring the fourfold if it is to grip us sufficiently to awaken us. The valuation ofnonhumans must stem from an ambient sensibility practiced in everyday comportment, not as an imposition on the world or an assignment of value, but as emergent through everyday disclosive practices. This is an ontological claim: affectability must itself be worldly; it must go beyond human doing. Latour's representationalist parliament sneaks humanism back in precisely where it seemed to have been evacuated. The simple fact of inclusion is insufficient to grant things existential weight and import. There can be no simple revaluing without deeper transformations in our lived relations to the world in ways that in turn attune us differently to world, that is, that bring the world as world to us otherwise than it now is for us.\n\nOur Worlds, Ourselves: Fourfold Dwelling",
