    {
      "raw_rank": 5,
      "chunk_id": "ab2442b48417ead0:00153",
      "distance": 0.8634017109870911,
      "path_rel": "rhetorical_ontology/Rickert, Thomas - Ambient Rhetoric- The Attunements of Rhetorical Being_(2013)_[My Copy].pdf",
      "page_start": 258,
      "page_end": 258,
      "text": "Walkin' down the street, smoggy-eyed Looking at the sky, starry-eyed Searchin' for the place, weary-eyed Crying in the night, teary-eyed\n\nDon't you know that it's true That for me and for you The world is a ghetto\n\nOn the one hand, the song could not be more different from Foghat's: here we see the city attuned through sadness and weariness, not celebration. And yet, as the song's later turn toward the theme oflove indicates, there is affirmation here, too.'3 Human beings may look to even an imperfect city when pursuing what a home might be. Indeed, War traces the fourfold in the opening verse, evoking earth, sky, mortals, and in the calling and searching, even some sense of the divine as essential to the placeness that as yet provides no home. The world remains a ghetto, marked by poverty and divisiveness, with home yet to be achieved. Most of War's members were ymmgblack men involved in the racial struggles of their time and thus also sensitive to the deep, racially drawn lines of segregation and poverty constituting the inner city, the ghetto, which adds further gravity both to their charge that the world is a ghetto and to their affirmation of place in the face of its poverty. The fourfold remains present even in the heart of strife-not, as I will show, in order to foster simple accommodation but rather to help kindle a coming together within and across difference. War's ghetto is simultaneously an indictment of what \"home\" is and a call to see that a \"home\" must still be sought, without, perhaps, knowing in advance precisely what that will be. And note the full scope of the charge that the world is ghetto: where the social and material divisiveness of poverty and racial prejudice exist, there can be no home, only ghetto, and this cannot be confined to one place-as, in fact, the notion of ghetto is meant to indicatebut rather encompasses all the world.14 From this charge comes the possibility ofawakeningthe need for a deeper, richer, more inclusive sense ofhome.15 Still, as I will show in exploring Heidegger's fourfold further, such a transformation cannot come solely from human actions or sources but rather stems from a deepening sensitivity to the nonhuman aspects of inhabitancy.\n\nOn this note I return to Heidegger: as is often the case with his writing, his stylistic choices, such as the diction throughout the farmhouse passage. bear import beyond the aesthetic. Who built the farmhouse? Dwelling built the farmhouse, not the peasants per se. How can this be if, as common sense tells us, it is humans who make and do things? How can we shift the action to dwelling, as ifwe have abdicated human agency in favor of an environmental. transhuman process? Heidegger's language, even in translation, accomplishes remarkable work. It indicates that while dwelling includes building, such activity is not the work ofhumans alone. Dwelling is activity conditioned by the land and the things of the world. Location in regard to the spring and the mountain, the weight of snow, the severity of weather, mortality, and more emerge as vital things in their own rights; they are transhuman powers manifesting themselves as the land's affordances and recalcitrances. This example recapitulates the theme that the being of beings cannot be adequately captured within a subject-object framework in which a subjective doer acts against an objective environment. Rather, the environment also takes part in the doing, and dwelling is Heidegger's distillation for this dispersion of agency. Dwelling thus requires less a subjective valuing of these things than a simple letting things be, not as passivity, but as a concerted action that hearkens to what things call forth (Heidegger, BW251; DT 56). This point recalls \"The World Is a Ghetto\": a ghetto is not simply a social problem of class and urban space; it includes finances, housing, planning, zoning, division.laws, struggle, selection, care and its absence, love, hate, and more. That is, the ghetto involves complex assemblages of humans and nonhumans, caught up in forms of mutual if asymmetrical disclosure. A ghetto is paradoxically born of both ambience and a disregard for it. Thus, War's song illuminates Heidegger in that it both evokes the richness of dwelling and condemns the assaults that despoil it.\n\n.....",
